# The Departed Valiant Tamil
Tamil title: மறைந்த மறத்தமிழர்

Even those acquainted with him after a single meeting would understand. More than those who shared his views, it was those who differed from him who could never forget his name or his appearance.

A fox may hide when it sees a lion. But if one lion itself fears another, we must say that the power of the lion that inspires such fear is great. M. Arangasamy lived with such courage of heart, physical strength, and devotion to principle.

He was not one who studied for many years in school and obtained a degree. What of it? Do we not see even today many university graduates who retain faith in imagined gods and minds belonging to the Stone Age? He had long possessed fierce Vaishnavite devotion; that he transformed himself into a radical in every sphere of life is indeed remarkable.

As far as Kanchipuram is concerned, it was he who established the Self-Respect Movement there. This happened fifteen years ago. At that time, among those who gathered at meetings, those who threw stones from hiding and those who openly hurled every kind of abuse were the more numerous. With the sacred sectarian mark shining across his broad forehead, it was he who gave strength and encouragement to the rare and great efforts of a few young men!

He saw no distinction between torn clothing and glittering attire. He lived always as a companion of the poor. Not a trace of the habit of grinning at moneybags existed in him. He did not know that there was such a word as "impossible." Fear was a quality unknown to him.

It will be difficult for the Kanchi Municipal Council ever to find another man like him. He was also a member of the Chingleput District Board and worked in many other public institutions. In no place did he ever forget the welfare of the people who had sent him there.

In several attempts made to bring him down by seeking police aid because of differences of opinion, he emerged victorious. He spent his entire life like a bull that had entered the battlefield.

He was a true friend to Muslim comrades. To the community into which he was born, the Vanniyakula Kshatriyas, he stood as a staff to the blind and a light in darkness. The anguish he experienced on seeing the lack of education and poverty among his people cannot be confined to writing.

He was only fifty-four. When his heart unexpectedly stopped, he died at eight in the evening on 16 March 1947. Thousands of people gathered. Many spoke, each from what he knew, about the distinctions of his life and grieved.

Two rows of two hundred black flags accompanied the guileless body of the valiant Tamil, borne to the cremation ground upon a bier like a wooden vessel. More than five thousand people followed. A vast crowd gathered at the cremation ground. A. K. Thangavelar presided. Comrade Annadurai, his voice trembling, spoke a few words about the special qualities of the departed elder and stopped. Comrades Kallipattu Krishnasamy Naicker, Officer V. Kannan, Aiyamperumal, M. Devarajan, Kanchi Kalyanasundaram, and others spoke briefly. The chairman's concluding words withered every heart. Thousands upon thousands of eyes shed tears. The hero's body was then set alight.

The Tamil hero's body burned and became ash, but the history of his valour will never disappear.

Never before had the people of Kanchipuram paid such honour to anyone who had died. From this alone it is perfectly clear how deeply the late M. Arangasamy was loved by the people.

The death of the valiant Tamil is a great loss to the public and an unforgettable grief. For his family it is suffering beyond words. His only son is a boy whose moustache has not yet begun to sprout. We have no power to console his broken heart, the stunned mind of the departed man's daughter, the scorched heart of his aged mother, and the trembling hearts of elders, younger people, kinsmen, relatives, and many others, because our own heart is in the same state.

Yet, in the spirit of consoling one another, we convey our deepest sympathy to all of them. A mountain has fallen! What can be done? The hills must now become mountains. We must work without forgetting the virtues of the departed.

*Seithi, Dravida Nadu, 29 March 1942*

---NOTES---
The first surviving scan begins mid-article and omits the printed heading; the descriptive title follows the filename and the article's repeated மறத்தமிழர். The body clearly prints the death date as 16-3-47, while the closing citation clearly prints 29-3-42. Both readings are preserved without silently reconciling the chronological conflict.
